<header><p class="st-kicker">other · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Madras Presidency</h1><p class="st-meta">1652 CE – 1947 CE · capital Madras</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British province of southern India governed from Fort St George at Madras, the East India Company's oldest presidency.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1639 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">English East India Company</a> obtained land at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madraspatnam</a> from a local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a> and began building <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-st-george">Fort St George</a> there the following year. In 1652 the settlement was raised to the status of a presidency, independent of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/banten-sultanate">Bantam</a>. Victories over the French in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnatic-wars">Carnatic Wars</a> and over the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore</a> in 1799 extended it across most of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A governor and council ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, under the Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> from 1773. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-munro">Thomas Munro</a> introduced the ryotwari system, assessing land revenue directly on peasant cultivators. The presidency covered Tamil, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telugu-language">Telugu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malayalam-language">Malayalam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> areas. Railways, irrigation such as the Kaveri anicuts, universities and newspapers spread, while famines, notably in 1876 to 1878, caused great loss of life.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> became a province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/british-raj">British India</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a>, gained elected ministries from 1920 and joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dominion-of-india">Dominion of India</a> in 1947 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madras-state">Madras State</a>. It was divided on linguistic lines from 1953 to 1956, and the remainder was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> in 1969.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/madras-presidency.jpg" alt="Madras Presidency" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: UnknownBharatiya87 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blue_Ensign_of_the_Madras_Presidency.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1652 CE – 1947 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Madras</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Elihu Yale, Thomas Munro</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Islam, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tamil, Telugu, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Presidency of Fort St George, Madras Province</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1800&amp;focus=madras-presidency" aria-label="Madras Presidency on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6882 1690 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Madras Presidency lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7202 1946L4997 651M7202 1946L7364 1601M7202 1946L7364 1602"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7202 1946h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7202 1946h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1800&amp;focus=madras-presidency">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

